/* Track: What you control and what you forecast. Ordered foundational first.
 *
 * Your terminal wealth is a function of a set you set and a set you can only
 * predict, and almost all of the attention goes to the second one. So this
 * track opens on the distinction, installs the controllable decisions once,
 * and spends the rest of its entries on what the evidence actually says about
 * trying to buy your way into the forecastable half. */
(window.LESSONS = window.LESSONS || []).push(
{
  id: "costs-controllable-and-forecastable",
  track: "costs", level: "judgement",
  title: "Your outcome is a set you control plus a set you forecast, and nearly all your attention goes to the half you cannot set",
  source: "The separation of decision variables from state variables, as control engineering and operations research use it, applied to a household balance sheet",
  gateIntro: "It is Sunday morning and you have two tabs open. One is a forum thread about whether the market is expensive at these levels, forty-one replies deep, and you have read most of them. The other is the fee page of the fund holding most of your money, which you opened by accident in February and closed again. Fifty minutes have gone into the first tab and none into the second, and someone has just asked what you are doing.",
  gatePrompt: "Write the instruction that would change something on the second tab, with an amount and a date in it, before you go back to the first.",
  evidence: "No trial. This is a way of carving up the problem rather than a claim about the world, borrowed from the ordinary engineering habit of describing a system in terms of the variables you set and the variables that are merely done to you. Nobody has randomised households into sorting their decisions this way and it is hard to imagine the design. What can be said is narrower and comes from the entries that follow: every quantity in the controllable list has been shown somewhere to move terminal wealth, and no method of forecasting the other list has survived out-of-sample testing well enough to earn the attention it takes. Treat the framing as a filing system whose merit is that it is cheap to apply, not as evidence of anything.",
  replication: "craft",
  idea: "Sort every financial decision into the things you set and the things you can only predict, then notice which pile your last six months went into.",
  why: "The first pile is short and you can write it out. How much you put away. What you pay per year to hold it, all in. What legal structure it sits in. How often you trade. The allocation you picked. Whether the arrangement is still running next year. Every item there is a number you set today, and you know its value the moment you set it.\n\nThe second pile has one item that matters and it is returns, along with its relatives: inflation, the order in which the good and bad years arrive, the currency, the property cycle. You can narrow the distribution on these with real work. You cannot collapse it, and the width that remains is wider than almost any effect you were hoping to find.\n\nSo effort buys different goods in the two piles. An hour in the first produces a known change to a known quantity that persists until you reverse it. An hour in the second produces an estimate with an error bar around it and a shelf life measured in months. Attention flows the wrong way anyway, because the second pile is the interesting one: it has news, argument, other people's opinions and the possibility of turning out to have been right.\n\nThe usable test is whether the thinking terminates. Anything in the first pile can be finished this afternoon and then left alone. Anything in the second is never finished, which is exactly why it can absorb a decade.",
  failureMode: "Six years of reading, a defensible view on where rates were going that was right twice, and a fee on your largest balance you have never once looked up. The forecasting was the enjoyable part and it was also the part that could never have been banked. The fee was the part you could have set on a Tuesday afternoon and never thought about again.",
  worked: "The instruction is a filter, and it goes on paper rather than into an app:\n\n\"Before I spend an hour on anything financial, write at the top of the page which pile it belongs to. If it is a forecast, the hour is entertainment and I will call it that. If it is a control, the hour ends with something switched on.\"\n\nThen the one control to switch on first, because it is the same for nearly everybody:\n\n\"Look up the total percentage per year I am paying on my largest balance, write it down with today's date, and book one afternoon this month to change it.\"\n\nWhat is deliberately absent is any prohibition on the forecasting. Banning the interesting half does not work and does not need to. The filter only has to stop you from filing it under progress.",
  experiment: "Write down the last three financial things you thought hard about, and beside each one write set or predicted. Then write down the last three things you actually changed. Most people find the first list long, the second list short, and almost no overlap between them. Take one item from the predicted column and find its control-pile equivalent, which is usually sitting directly underneath it: the fee inside the fund whose returns you were forecasting, the transfer underneath the savings target. Do that one instead, today.",
  reflection: "Which of the last three things you thought about could have been finished, and did you finish it?",
  forecast: { q: "Will the next hour I spend on money end with something switched on rather than an opinion formed?" },
  recall: {
    q: "Both piles affect your terminal wealth, so why is it a mistake to give them proportionate effort?",
    a: "Because an hour buys a different thing in each. In the controllable pile it buys a known change to a known quantity that holds until you reverse it, and the decision is then closed. In the forecastable pile it buys an estimate whose error bar is wider than the effect you were chasing, and the decision never closes, so it can consume attention indefinitely.\n\nThe test is termination. Financial thinking that can be completed this afternoon and then left alone is a decision. Thinking that will still be open next year was never a decision, it was a view."
  },
  deepDive: "Here is everything I have thought about my money in the last six months. Sort it into things I set and things I predicted, and tell me which control I left undefended while I was busy with the predictions."
},
{
  id: "costs-net-is-gross-minus-cost",
  track: "costs", level: "standing",
  title: "Active management must underperform passive in aggregate by exactly its cost, and that is arithmetic rather than evidence",
  source: "William Sharpe, The Arithmetic of Active Management (Financial Analysts Journal, 1991), and John Bogle's cost matters hypothesis",
  gateIntro: "A colleague has been running their own share picks for four years, is ahead of the index, and has the spreadsheet open on their second monitor to prove it. You believe the spreadsheet. You also could not say what you pay per year on the account holding most of your money, and when they ask what you are in you say a low-cost index fund and quietly hope the word low is doing some work.",
  gatePrompt: "Write the instruction that finds your own total annual percentage and caps it, with a number in it, before you argue with anybody's spreadsheet.",
  evidence: "No trial, and none is possible. Sharpe's argument is an identity: define passive as holding every security in proportion to its market value, notice that the market is the sum of all holdings, and it follows that the return on the average actively managed dollar equals the return on the average passively managed dollar before costs, and must be lower after them. Nothing is measured, so nothing can fail to replicate. The identity binds only in aggregate and only under its definitions. It says nothing about any individual manager, it needs the passive benchmark to be the whole market rather than an index that leaves part of it out, and closet indexers and leveraged positions sit awkwardly inside the accounting. Bogle's cost matters hypothesis is the same statement pointed at the investor rather than at the industry. Both are constraints the evidence has to satisfy, not evidence.",
  replication: "craft",
  idea: "Net return is gross return minus cost, which means the only term in your net return that is knowable today is the one you subtract.",
  why: "The identity binds because holdings have to add up. Every security is owned by somebody, so the value-weighted average of all portfolios is the market itself. If one actively managed dollar beats the market, another actively managed dollar has to lose by the same amount. Before costs the whole activity is zero sum, and after costs it is negative sum by the size of the costs. There is no configuration of clever people in which this stops being true.\n\nWhat makes it more than a debating point is which side of the equation you can observe. Expected gross return is a distribution with a wide interval around it and you will not know this year's draw until the year is over. The fee is printed in a document you can open now. So cost is not merely one of several factors. It is the only term you can set, and every basis point you take out of it arrives in your net return with a coefficient of exactly one and no forecast attached.\n\nThe identity does not say that no manager can win, and it does not say you cannot be the one. It says that the average of the group you would be joining is the market minus what that group pays to play, so the prior you walk in with is the group average, and the entire burden falls on the claim that you have selected yourself out of it. The next several entries are about how well that claim holds up when somebody checks.",
  failureMode: "You accept the argument in principle and go on paying a spread you have never measured. Twenty-five years of a fee you did not look up is not a forecast that went against you. It is a subtraction you agreed to in advance and then stopped thinking about, and unlike the returns it was never going to surprise you in the good direction.",
  worked: "Two lines, and the first is only a lookup:\n\n\"Write down the total percentage per year I pay on each account: investment fee plus administration fee plus platform or brokerage cost, added into one number per account, with today's date beside it.\"\n\n\"Set my own ceiling: I hold nothing whose total annual cost is above that number unless I have written on the same page what I am buying for the excess.\"\n\nYou pick the ceiling yourself, because a limit somebody else chose is one you will negotiate with. What is deliberately absent is any mention of performance. Cost is a figure you can read off a document this afternoon; performance is a figure you would have to forecast, and putting both in one instruction turns a ten-minute lookup into a research project that never gets done.",
  experiment: "Open the product disclosure statement or fee page for whichever account holds the most of your money, and add three numbers: the investment fee, the administration fee, and whatever the platform or broker takes. Write the total down. Very few people who have read a book about index funds can state that number from memory for their own largest holding, and that gap is the whole subject of this entry. Then write your ceiling beside it.",
  reflection: "What was the total, and had you ever added those numbers together before today?",
  recall: {
    q: "Sharpe's arithmetic is not an empirical result, so how does it constrain what the empirical results are allowed to say?",
    a: "Because it follows from the definitions. If passive means holding every security in proportion to its market value, and the market is the sum of all holdings, then the average active dollar matches the average passive dollar before costs and falls short after them. No study can overturn that. A study that appeared to has redefined a term, usually by benchmarking against an index that is not the whole market.\n\nWhat it constrains is interpretation. Evidence of a skilled manager is not evidence against the identity, because the identity is a statement about the average of the group. It shifts the burden onto the claim that you have selected out of that average, and it makes cost the only term in the equation you get to set rather than predict."
  },
  deepDive: "Here are the fee pages for my accounts. Add up my real all-in annual cost on each, tell me which one is worst relative to what it is actually doing for me, and give me a ceiling I could still defend to myself in a year."
},
{
  id: "costs-expenses-predict-net-return",
  track: "costs", level: "standing",
  title: "Fees and turnover predict a fund's shortfall close to one for one, while past returns predict almost nothing except at the bottom",
  source: "Mark Carhart, On Persistence in Mutual Fund Performance (Journal of Finance, 1997)",
  gateIntro: "The annual statement arrives and the three-year figure is above the benchmark, printed in the largest type on the page. The cost sits on page two inside a table of four rows. The portfolio turnover is on page three and you have never known what to do with it. Your renewal falls due in a fortnight and the covering letter says no action is required.",
  gatePrompt: "Write the instruction that decides what you hold using only the figures on pages two and three.",
  evidence: "A survivorship-bias-free panel of United States diversified equity mutual funds running from the early nineteen sixties to the early nineties, several thousand funds, with returns run through a four-factor model that added momentum to market, size and value. Two results. Cost variables predicted net return: expense ratios came through against performance at close to one for one, turnover carried a further cost on top, and load funds did worse again. And almost all the apparent persistence in fund returns turned out to be momentum in the underlying stocks rather than manager skill, with the only strong persistence sitting in the worst-performing funds, which reliably stayed bad. One country, one asset class, and a window that closes before both the industry's fee structure and the passive alternative changed substantially. The cost half is what has since been reproduced across markets, fund types and later periods, which is why it is treated as settled here and the rest of the paper is not.",
  replication: "replicated",
  cheat: "Rank funds by cost, not by past return: expense ratios predict net underperformance close to one for one, and the only reliable persistence is bad funds staying bad.",
  idea: "The two figures on a factsheet that actually predict what you will earn are the fee and the turnover, and the headline return predicts almost nothing.",
  why: "Cost comes through at close to one for one for a boring reason. A fee is subtracted from whatever the portfolio earned, so the only way a dearer manager avoids delivering a correspondingly worse net result is by producing more gross return to cover it. A coefficient near one is what a regression says when they do not. The finding looks empirical and is mostly mechanical, which is what makes it so stable across periods and countries.\n\nTurnover is the part people miss, because it is charged twice and disclosed once. The explicit brokerage and the spread come out of the portfolio without ever appearing in the expense ratio, and if the holding sits outside a shelter, every sale also crystallises a tax liability that would otherwise have gone on deferring itself. So the published fee is not the cost. It is the part of the cost that the industry agreed to print.\n\nThe persistence result creates a sharp asymmetry in how you may use the largest number on the page. If winners do not repeat but losers do, then past return is close to useless as a reason to buy and genuinely useful as a reason to sell. That is not the same as saying nobody has skill, and the later entries take that question seriously. It is a statement about the signal in a three-year number, which is so poor that stock momentum accounts for most of what looked like a manager.",
  failureMode: "You move into last year's top-quartile fund and pay forty basis points more for it. The performance does not repeat, because most of what you were buying was momentum in the underlying holdings that has already rotated. The forty points repeat every year for as long as you own it, and eleven years later the fee difference is the only part of that decision still measurable.",
  worked: "The buy rule and the sell rule are deliberately different in kind:\n\n\"When choosing between funds that hold roughly the same thing, sort by total annual cost and buy the cheapest one that holds what I actually want. Do not open the performance tab.\"\n\n\"Once a year, check whether anything I own has trailed the thing it is meant to track by more than its fee for three years running. If it has, sell it.\"\n\nWhat is absent is any rule that buys on past performance, and the asymmetry comes straight out of the finding rather than out of temperament. Winners do not repeat reliably enough to act on. Losers do.",
  experiment: "Pull up the fund or exchange traded fund holding most of your money and find three figures on the same document: total annual cost, portfolio turnover, and how far it has drifted from the index it tracks. If turnover is not disclosed at all, that absence is itself worth noting. Then find the two cheapest alternatives holding approximately the same thing and write down the cost difference per year against your actual balance. That difference is the only part of the comparison you can be confident about in advance.",
  reflection: "Which figure had you actually been deciding on, and which one did you have to go looking for?",
  recall: {
    q: "Expense ratios predict net return at close to one for one. Why is that nearer to arithmetic than to a discovery, and what was the discovery?",
    a: "Because the fee is subtracted from the return. The only way a higher fee could fail to reduce the net result one for one is if dearer managers produced correspondingly higher gross returns, and a coefficient near one is the regression's way of reporting that they do not. Once price is unrelated to gross performance, the pass-through is mechanical.\n\nThe discovery was the persistence result. Almost all the apparent repeat performance was momentum in the underlying stocks rather than skill, and the only strong persistence sat among the worst funds. That makes past return usable as a sell signal and close to useless as a buy signal, while cost is visible today and barely moves."
  },
  deepDive: "Here are the funds I hold with their costs and turnover figures. Rank them on the part of the comparison that is knowable in advance, and tell me which one I am paying up for on the strength of a three-year number."
},
{
  id: "costs-net-alpha-looks-like-luck",
  track: "costs", level: "judgement",
  title: "The spread of managers' net results is about what pure luck would produce, so very few of them cover their own costs",
  source: "Eugene Fama and Kenneth French, Luck versus Skill in the Cross-Section of Mutual Fund Returns (Journal of Finance, 2010)",
  gateIntro: "A fund you have heard of has beaten its benchmark nine years running, and the person telling you about it has worked out how unlikely that is for a manager with no ability at all. The number they got is very small and you cannot fault their arithmetic. You also cannot remember how many funds there are in that category, and nobody in the conversation has raised it.",
  gatePrompt: "Write the instruction that settles what you hold without needing to know whether that particular manager is any good.",
  evidence: "United States equity mutual funds across roughly half a century, several thousand of them, analysed with a bootstrap rather than a single regression, and the method is the finding. They simulated what the cross-section of estimated alphas would look like if every manager's true alpha were zero, then held that simulated distribution up against the real one. The real distribution of net alphas sat close to the simulated one, with a thinner right tail than genuine skill would generate and a fatter left tail, and the reading was that very few managers produce enough gross return to pay for their costs. Before costs the picture is less bleak, which is the gap the next entry walks into. Limitations: one country, one fund type, aggregate returns rather than holdings, and conclusions that depend on the factor model chosen to define alpha in the first place. It also cannot name anybody. It is a statement about the shape of a distribution.",
  replication: "single",
  idea: "Simulate a world in which no manager has any ability and you get roughly the distribution of fund performance we actually observe, which is why an individual track record cannot be read as evidence of anything.",
  why: "The question people ask about a good record is the wrong one. Asking how unlikely nine winning years would be for a manager with no ability treats the record as if it had arrived on its own. It did not. It was selected out of a population of thousands, and in a population that size a handful of long runs is not merely possible under chance, it is guaranteed. The answerable question is how many records like this one should exist if nobody could do it, and a bootstrap answers exactly that.\n\nThe shape of the two tails is where the cost interpretation lives. There were fewer outstanding funds than real skill would produce and more disastrous ones than luck alone accounts for. That is what a subtraction does to a distribution: shift the whole thing left by the fee and the right tail thins while the left tail fattens. So the result is not quite the flat claim that skill does not exist. It is the narrower and more damaging claim that there is not enough of it, on average, to pay for itself.\n\nWhich forces an uncomfortable position on anybody choosing. If the returns of the lucky and the returns of the able are the same object, no amount of staring at returns separates them beforehand, and you will not find out within a horizon that helps you. The only honest stance is to treat the purchase as a draw from that distribution. There is exactly one thing you can do to a random draw, which is to move the whole distribution right by shrinking the amount taken out of it.",
  failureMode: "You put a quarter of the portfolio behind a nine-year record. The record was real. It was also one of the several dozen nine-year records that would exist in a population that size if nobody could pick stocks at all, you had no way to tell those apart, and you will not have one for another nine years, over which the fee gets paid nine more times.",
  worked: "The instruction is a rule about what evidence you will accept from yourself:\n\n\"I do not buy anything on the strength of a track record. If I want an active holding it is capped at ten percent of the portfolio, it is funded out of new contributions rather than by selling the core, and the reason is written down in one sentence before I buy it.\"\n\nThe cap and the sentence do different jobs. The cap bounds what being wrong costs. The sentence exists so that in three years you can check whether you were right for the reason you actually gave, rather than for a reason you constructed afterwards.\n\nWhat is deliberately absent is a prohibition. A rule that bans the interesting thing outright tends to get broken all at once, and at the worst possible moment.",
  experiment: "Take the best track record you currently find persuasive and go and find out how many funds sit in its category. Then ask roughly how many nine-year runs a population that size would throw up under pure chance. You do not need the exact figure. You only need to notice whether you had ever asked the question before you found the record convincing. Then write your active cap as a percentage, today, in the same note as your fee ceiling.",
  reflection: "What is your active cap, and did you set it before or after you last looked at a track record?",
  recall: {
    q: "Fama and French did not test whether any particular manager has skill. What did they test instead, and why is that the more useful question?",
    a: "They simulated the entire cross-section of alphas that would appear if every manager's true alpha were zero, and compared it with the cross-section actually observed. The real distribution of net alphas sat close to the simulated one, with a thinner right tail and a fatter left tail than chance alone would give.\n\nIt is more useful because in a population of thousands, impressive individual records are certain to exist under pure chance, so asking whether this record could have been luck is unanswerable in isolation. The shape of the tails is answerable, and the shape says the average manager generates roughly enough gross return to be indistinguishable from noise and not enough to pay the fee."
  },
  deepDive: "I hold one actively managed thing and I can still remember why I bought it. Argue the other side against my actual holding, and tell me what I would have to see in the next three years to conclude I was wrong rather than merely unlucky."
},
{
  id: "costs-skill-exists-and-is-captured",
  track: "costs", level: "judgement",
  title: "Skill in funds management is real and measurable, and the manager captures it through fund size rather than handing it to you",
  source: "Jonathan Berk and Jules van Binsbergen, Measuring skill in the mutual fund industry (Journal of Financial Economics, 2015)",
  gateIntro: "You have just finished arguing, fairly convincingly, that nobody in funds management can actually do the job, and the person across the table asks why the industry is worth what it is worth then. Your answer requires several thousand professional institutional investors to have been fooled for forty years, and you notice, mid-sentence, that you do not really believe that.",
  gatePrompt: "Write the instruction that stands up whether or not skill turns out to exist, and name what it is protecting you from.",
  evidence: "United States active mutual funds over several decades, measured differently from the net-alpha literature and that is the entire argument. Berk and van Binsbergen hold that a percentage alpha is the wrong yardstick, because a manager who can reliably extract a fixed dollar amount will show a shrinking percentage as the fund grows, so they measure value added instead: gross alpha multiplied by assets under management, in dollars, against tradeable index funds rather than an academic factor model. On that measure the average fund adds a positive amount, more of the cross-section does so than chance would generate, and it persists over horizons where percentage alpha does not. They also find more value added where investors reward past performance with flows, which is the mechanism by which the surplus gets taken. Limitations: the result rests on the benchmark choice, the investor's net position comes back to roughly nothing once fees are on the other side of the ledger, and it still cannot name a skilled manager in advance. It is a live disagreement with the previous entry rather than a refutation of it.",
  replication: "contested",
  idea: "The evidence that managers have no ability and the evidence that they have plenty are measuring different quantities, and both are consistent with you earning nothing at all for hiring one.",
  why: "Start with the equilibrium logic, because it dissolves the apparent contradiction. Suppose some managers can genuinely add value. Investors are not blind, so money flows toward them, and the fund grows until the good ideas are spread across a base too large for them to move the percentage return any more. At that size the excess return to the investor is gone, and it is gone precisely because the ability was real and was recognised. Zero net alpha is what a world with scarce genuine skill predicts, not a world without it.\n\nThat is why the measurement choice decides the answer. A percentage divides the ability by the size of the fund, so a manager who extracts a steady dollar amount looks progressively worse the more successful they become, and a career of real value creation reads as a slow decline. Value added in dollars has no such property. Two credible teams look at the same funds, one measuring the rate and one measuring the quantity, and reach opposite conclusions without either being careless.\n\nHere is the part that matters for you, and it is the same under both readings. If there is no ability, there is nothing to buy. If there is ability, it has a market price, that price is set by every other investor competing for the same capacity, and you pay it away in fees and in the dilution that arrives with the flows. The skill is not for sale to you on terms that leave you better off, which is a stronger and stranger conclusion than the folk version.\n\nWhat does change is which claim you should be standing on. Anybody defending an index portfolio with the assertion that nobody can beat the market has built on ground that a single good manager can take away. Build on the price instead, and the portfolio survives being shown a twenty-year record.",
  failureMode: "You win the argument at dinner using the no-ability claim, and three years later somebody produces a manager with a defensible twenty-year record and a mechanism you cannot dismiss. Your position collapses because it was resting on the wrong foundation, and you move a third of the portfolio into a fund whose capacity was sold years ago to institutions who got there before you and who pay less than you will.",
  worked: "The instruction has to be indifferent to which side of the argument wins:\n\n\"My core allocation sits in the cheapest broad index exposure available to me, and the reason recorded in the file is the price of skill rather than the absence of it.\"\n\nThen the test that keeps the reason honest:\n\n\"If I ever pay above my cost ceiling, I have to write down what I am buying and why the person selling it has not already sold its capacity to somebody much larger than me.\"\n\nWhat is deliberately absent is the claim most people use to justify exactly this portfolio. Resting it on the absence of ability leaves you defenceless the day somebody shows you ability, and the portfolio never needed that claim in the first place.",
  experiment: "Write down, in one sentence, why your core holding is what it is. If that sentence turns out to be a version of nobody can beat the market, rewrite it as a sentence about price and capacity, and notice that the holding itself does not change at all. That is the whole exercise. A correct decision resting on a fragile reason gets abandoned the first time the reason is attacked, and it will be attacked by somebody holding a track record.",
  reflection: "What reason had you written down for your core holding, and did it survive being rewritten?",
  recall: {
    q: "How can it be true both that fund investors earn roughly nothing above the benchmark and that fund managers have real, persistent ability?",
    a: "Because ability is scarce and money chases it. Flows arrive until the fund is large enough that the excess return per dollar has been competed away, and the surplus is taken by the manager in fees and by the sheer size of the fund. Zero net alpha to investors is the equilibrium that a model containing real skill predicts, not a refutation of skill.\n\nThe measurement dispute follows from that. A percentage divides the ability by the assets, so a manager extracting a steady dollar amount looks worse as they succeed, while value added in dollars does not. That is how two credible teams read the same funds and disagree, and it leaves the investor in the same place either way: the skill has a price and other people have already paid it."
  },
  deepDive: "I want to stress-test the reason I hold index funds rather than the holding itself. Attack my written reason, and tell me what a manager would have to demonstrate for that reason to stop applying."
},
{
  id: "costs-majority-trails-the-benchmark",
  track: "costs", level: "standing",
  title: "A majority of active funds trail their own benchmark over long horizons, in every market where somebody has repeated the comparison",
  source: "The S&P Indices Versus Active scorecards and the Morningstar Active-Passive Barometer, both published on a repeating schedule across multiple regions including Australia",
  gateIntro: "The annual letter says your fund has had a respectable decade. What the letter does not mention is the four funds in the same house that were closed or merged away over those ten years, one of which you held in 2019 and can no longer find anywhere on the website. Somebody at work asks whether active management works, and you find yourself citing your own statement as though it settled it.",
  gatePrompt: "Write the instruction that would have caught the fund you can no longer find, and say which week of the year it runs.",
  evidence: "Not a study but a repeated measurement, which is a different sort of strength. Both exercises take a fund universe, compare each fund against a stated benchmark over horizons stretching out to fifteen and twenty years, and publish again every six or twelve months across many markets. The result that survives every edition is directional: over long horizons a majority of active funds in most categories fail to beat the benchmark chosen for them, and the majority gets larger as the horizon lengthens. The figures are also constructed to count the whole starting cohort rather than only the funds still trading at the end, which makes them considerably less flattering than the industry averages you will see quoted elsewhere. Limits worth holding onto: the benchmark chosen for each category is contestable and it drives the entire result, both publishers have commercial positions, category boundaries move between editions, and none of this is a controlled test of anything. What makes it usable is that the direction has not reversed at the fifteen-year horizon in any market that publishes one.",
  replication: "replicated",
  cheat: "Buy the benchmark rather than a manager: over fifteen-year horizons a majority of active funds trail theirs, in every market where the comparison gets published.",
  idea: "You cannot know whether your fund will beat its benchmark, but the proportion that did over your horizon in your market is published, and that base rate is the only figure available to you before the fact.",
  why: "The useful object here is the base rate, not the verdict on any fund. Nobody can tell you whether the thing you hold will beat its yardstick over the next fifteen years. Somebody can tell you what share of comparable funds managed it over the last fifteen, in your market, counting the ones that did not survive. Treating your own holding as a draw from that published distribution is not cynicism about the manager. It is the only calculation that is actually available at the moment you are deciding.\n\nWhy the majority grows with the horizon is worth understanding, because none of the three reasons require managers to be bad at their jobs. The cost is levied every single year while good stretches are not annual. A single bad run is enough to lose a fifteen-year comparison and cannot be recovered by beating the yardstick slightly for longer afterwards, because the arithmetic of compounding is not symmetric. And funds are not permanent, so the horizon itself thins the field. Time is not a neutral referee in this contest. It is on the benchmark's side.\n\nThe honest use of these reports is therefore directional rather than numerical. Change the yardstick from a broad index to a category average and the answer moves, which is why the number that gets quoted at a barbecue is usually the one that suited whoever was quoting it. Repeat the direction, repeat that it steepens with horizon, and do not repeat a percentage.",
  failureMode: "Ten years of paying a spread over the index because the fund you hold was in the group that beat it for the first four. The comparison you were actually running all that time was the fund against its own history, which always looks like something is happening. The yardstick is the only comparison the fee has to beat, and it was in the disclosure document the whole time.",
  worked: "One annual instruction with a named screen and a named number:\n\n\"Every year in the first week of August, for each fund I hold, write down its return, the return of the benchmark named in its own disclosure document, and the difference. Three consecutive years of trailing by more than its fee and it goes.\"\n\nThen the standing one that makes the annual one mostly ceremonial:\n\n\"New contributions go to the cheapest broad index exposure by default. Anything else has to be argued for in writing, on the day, before the money moves.\"\n\nWhat is deliberately absent is the fund's own past as the comparison. A fund measured against itself always looks like it is working, and the three-year rule is there so that one bad year does not trigger a sale that the arithmetic does not justify.",
  experiment: "Find the benchmark named in your own fund's disclosure document rather than the one you assume it uses, and write both ten-year figures side by side. Do it for every fund you hold; it takes about twenty minutes for a normal portfolio. Then put a recurring calendar entry in the same week next year with the words already written into the title, because the value of this comparison lies entirely in its being repeated on a schedule instead of whenever you happen to feel curious.",
  reflection: "How many of your holdings beat the benchmark named in their own documents, and did you know the answer before you looked?",
  recall: {
    q: "The share of active funds trailing their benchmark rises as the horizon lengthens. Give the reasons, and say why the direction is safer to repeat than the number.",
    a: "Because the cost is charged every year while good stretches are not annual, because one bad run can lose a fifteen-year comparison and cannot be made up by small outperformance afterwards, and because not all funds survive the horizon, so the horizon itself thins the field. Time is not neutral in the comparison; it works for the benchmark.\n\nThe direction is safer than the number because the benchmark chosen for each category is contestable and it drives the whole result, and because the publishers have commercial positions. What survives every edition, every region and every revision of the categories is the direction and the fact that it steepens with horizon."
  },
  deepDive: "Here are my holdings and the benchmarks named in their own disclosure documents. Turn the annual comparison into a checklist I can run in twenty minutes, and tell me which of them have chosen a flattering yardstick and what a fair one would be."
},
{
  id: "costs-identical-funds-different-fees",
  track: "costs", level: "standing",
  title: "People choosing between index funds that hold the same thing do not pick the cheapest, and showing them the fees only partly fixes it",
  source: "James Choi, David Laibson and Brigitte Madrian, Why Does the Law of One Price Fail? An Experiment on Index Mutual Funds (Review of Financial Studies, 2010)",
  gateIntro: "There are three exchange traded funds on your platform following the same broad index. You have held one since 2018, and you chose it because it was the one your brother-in-law happened to mention at a christening. The other two sit on the same screen, which is sorted alphabetically, and one of them costs less than yours. You have opened that screen perhaps thirty times since.",
  gatePrompt: "Write the instruction that picks between the three today, plus the rule that decides when you would ever switch again.",
  evidence: "An experiment on students at two elite United States universities, including MBA students, who allocated a portfolio across four index funds that all tracked the same large-company index. Because the funds followed one index, the only defensible basis for choosing among them was cost, and the great majority of subjects did not minimise it. Two further results are the ones that travel. Subjects put weight on the past-return figures in the prospectuses, which for four funds following a single index differ mostly because the funds were launched at different times. And a treatment that collected the fees onto a single sheet reduced the error without removing it. Limitations that matter: these are students in a laboratory with modest stakes rather than households moving real balances, the menu was constructed for the experiment, and the sample is about as financially literate a population as anyone could assemble, which is what makes the result awkward rather than reassuring.",
  replication: "single",
  idea: "The one decision in investing with an unambiguously correct answer is routinely got wrong by people who fully understand the question, which locates the failure in attention rather than in knowledge.",
  why: "What makes this experiment sharp is that it removes the usual defence. Any choice between active funds can be justified by appealing to an unknown future, so a bad choice is never identifiable as an error. Four funds following one index have the same gross return by construction. Net return is then a pure function of cost, and buying the dearer one is not a judgement that went against you. It is a mistake, with the right answer printed on the page you were holding.\n\nThe reason the mistake survives disclosure tells you what kind of failure it is. The prospectus is long, the fee is one line inside it, and the return figure is prominent and feels like it means something. A number that varies invites comparison even when its variation carries no information at all, and here the variation comes largely from launch dates. Putting every fee on one sheet helped, which shows that part of the problem is the cost of looking. It did not fix it, which shows the rest is the pull of the more interesting number.\n\nThis is also the most encouraging finding in the track, and it is worth saying why. Everything else here says the returns half of your outcome is unknowable and the cost half is merely knowable. This says the cost half is knowable, sitting on a screen you already have open, and still gets ignored by people who could pass an exam on it. That is a return available in exchange for nothing but twenty minutes of attention, once.",
  failureMode: "Eight years in a fund costing fifteen basis points more than the near-identical one two rows below it on the same screen. No forecast was involved and no extra risk was taken. The only input was that the list was sorted alphabetically on the afternoon you opened the account, and you have re-opened it thirty times since without ever re-sorting it.",
  worked: "\"Sort the platform's list by total annual cost, not by name and not by past return. Of the funds holding what I actually want, buy the cheapest. If two are within a basis point or two of each other, buy either one and stop.\"\n\nThen the switching rule, because otherwise the instruction above runs every time you log in:\n\n\"I re-check this once a year, in the same week as the benchmark comparison. Outside that week I do not switch between near-identical funds, because the tax and the spread on switching cost more than the difference I would be chasing.\"\n\nWhat is deliberately absent is any tolerance for the past-return column when the underlying holdings are the same. If two funds follow one index, a difference in their reported histories is information about when they launched, not about what they will do next.",
  experiment: "Open your platform, list every fund it offers that follows the same index as your largest holding, and sort by total annual cost. If yours is not the cheapest, work out the annual difference against your actual balance and decide today whether to move, counting what the move itself costs in tax and spread. Then, whichever way you decide, change the default sort order on that screen if the platform will remember it, so that the next thirty visits begin from cost.",
  reflection: "Was your holding the cheapest of the funds following its index, and how long had the alternative been sitting on the same screen?",
  forecast: { q: "Will my next fund purchase be made from a list I sorted by cost before I looked at any returns?" },
  recall: {
    q: "Why is a menu of four index funds following the same index a stronger test of investor behaviour than a menu of four active funds?",
    a: "Because it removes the defence. With active funds any choice can be justified by a view about the future, so a poor choice is never identifiable as an error. Four funds following one index have the same gross return by construction, so net return is a pure function of cost and the correct answer is printed on the page.\n\nThat is what makes the result uncomfortable rather than amusing. The subjects were about as financially literate a group as could be assembled and most still did not minimise the fee, and gathering every fee onto one sheet improved matters without fixing them. Part of the failure is the cost of looking, and the rest is the pull of a past-return figure whose variation carries no information."
  },
  deepDive: "Here is the list of funds my platform offers that follow the same index as my main holding. Tell me which is cheapest all in, what switching would cost me in tax and spread, and whether the difference is worth the transaction at my balance."
},
{
  id: "costs-allocation-explains-three-things",
  track: "costs", level: "annual",
  title: "Asset allocation explains ninety percent, forty percent or a hundred percent of performance, depending on which of three questions you asked",
  source: "Roger Ibbotson and Paul Kaplan, Does Asset Allocation Policy Explain 40, 90, or 100 Percent of Performance? (Financial Analysts Journal, 2000), against the reading usually given to Brinson, Hood and Beebower, Determinants of Portfolio Performance (Financial Analysts Journal, 1986)",
  gateIntro: "The slide says ninety-three point six percent, in a large font, above a pie chart. The next slide is about the firm's allocation service and what it costs per year. You have now seen a version of this slide at three different firms, always with the same figure and never once with a citation underneath it, and it is your turn to speak.",
  gatePrompt: "Write the instruction that sets your own allocation once, with the actual percentages in it and the date you will next look at them.",
  evidence: "Two papers and a misquotation living between them. Brinson, Hood and Beebower took a set of large United States pension plans and decomposed the variation in each plan's returns over time, finding that investment policy accounted for the overwhelming majority of it. That result concerns variation over time within a single portfolio, which is the narrowest of several available questions, and it became the industry's favourite statistic by being repeated as though it answered the broadest. Ibbotson and Kaplan separated the questions and answered all three: allocation policy explained roughly ninety percent of the variability of one fund's returns over time, roughly forty percent of the variation in returns between different funds, and close to all of the level of return, if anything slightly more than all of it once fees were taken out. Same data, three answers, three different meanings. Neither paper is a trial and neither can tell you what your own allocation ought to be. What is established is that the number quoted from the podium answers a question nobody in the room asked.",
  replication: "overclaimed",
  idea: "The famous statistic about allocation answers the narrowest of three possible questions, and the two answers that would actually bear on a fee are far less flattering to whoever is quoting it.",
  why: "Take the three questions apart, because the words are nearly identical and the meanings are not. First: how much of the movement in one portfolio's returns over time comes from its policy mix rather than from deviations around that mix? Nearly all of it, and the answer is close to tautological. When equities fall, a portfolio built mostly on equities falls with them, whether the mix was chosen by a committee or thrown at a dartboard and then left alone. That figure is a statement about markets moving together, not a measure of anybody's allocation skill.\n\nSecond: how much of the difference between your outcome and somebody else's comes from allocation? Much less, in the region of two fifths, with the remainder going to selection, timing and cost. This is the question that a fee has to answer, because it is the question of whether hiring somebody changes your result relative to what you would otherwise have held. It is also the least impressive of the three answers, which is why it is not the one on the slide.\n\nThird: how much of the level of your return comes from allocation? Close to all of it, and slightly more than all of it once fees come out, which is the answer worth keeping. If allocation accounts for essentially the entire level of return and the remaining activity nets to a little below zero, then the whole apparatus of selection and timing and tilting is being paid for out of a residual that is not there. That puts allocation in the controllable set as the single decision carrying the weight, and makes it something to settle once, cheaply, rather than a service to rent by the year.",
  failureMode: "Forty basis points a year for an allocation service, bought on the strength of a statistic about something else. The allocation it produced is perfectly reasonable and close to what a single diversified fund would have handed you for a fraction of the price. The figure that sold it would have been just as high if your mix had been chosen at random in 2015 and then never touched.",
  worked: "The instruction is the allocation itself, written down with numbers in it, because an allocation you cannot state is not one you hold:\n\n\"My allocation is seventy percent growth assets and thirty percent defensive, held in these two funds, and I next look at it on the first of July next year and not before.\"\n\nThen the sentence that makes it a decision rather than a drift:\n\n\"Any change to those percentages gets a date and one written reason, and a market movement is not a reason.\"\n\nWhat is deliberately absent is a target return. Writing one down converts an allocation into a forecast, and it then invites you to adjust the allocation every time the forecast disappoints, which is the opposite of what the third answer above tells you to do. The percentages and the review date are the entire instruction.",
  experiment: "Write your current allocation down as two or three percentages from memory, then go and check it against the actual balances. The gap between the remembered version and the real one is the interesting output, and for most people the real portfolio has drifted several points from the one they believe they own. Then write the review date beside it. If you have never written the percentages down at all, you do not currently have an allocation, you have a collection.",
  reflection: "What did you think your allocation was, and what was it?",
  recall: {
    q: "Allocation is said to explain ninety percent of performance. Which question does that answer, and which question should you be asking when somebody charges you for allocation?",
    a: "It answers how much of the movement in one portfolio's returns over time is attributable to its policy mix, and the answer is high largely because markets move together: a portfolio built on equities falls when equities fall, however the mix was arrived at. It is closer to a statement about correlation than a measure of allocation skill.\n\nThe question a fee has to answer is the between-portfolio one, which is how much of the difference between your outcome and somebody else's comes from allocation rather than from selection, timing and cost, and the answer there is around two fifths. The third answer is the one worth keeping: allocation accounts for close to the whole level of return, and slightly more once fees are removed, which says the rest of the activity nets out to a little below nothing."
  },
  deepDive: "Here are my actual balances. State my current allocation as percentages, tell me how far it has drifted from what I believe I hold, and give me the one sentence to write down so that next year's version of me does not renegotiate it."
},
{
  id: "costs-percentage-fee-compounds",
  track: "costs", level: "annual",
  title: "A percentage charge grows with your balance and a flat charge does not, so which provider is cheaper flips at a balance you can compute",
  source: "The arithmetic of proportional against fixed pricing, as it applies to the platform and administration layer rather than to the fund inside it",
  gateIntro: "Your platform takes a percentage of the balance with no upper limit, and it was comfortably the cheaper option when you opened the account in 2019. The balance has tripled since. The competitor charges a flat amount per month that looked ridiculous at the time, and you have not opened its pricing page since, and your latest annual statement is sitting in the same folder as the one from the year you signed up.",
  gatePrompt: "Write the instruction that works out which of the two is cheaper at today's balance, and name the balance at which you would move.",
  evidence: "No trial. This is division. A charge levied as a percentage of assets scales with the balance without limit unless the provider caps it, a flat charge does not scale at all, and the balance at which the two cross is the flat charge divided by the difference between the percentage rates. There is nothing here to replicate and no study to cite. What is worth stating is why the arithmetic gets skipped rather than got wrong: the comparison was made once, at a balance that no longer exists, and nothing in the system ever prompts a second look. The provider whose charge is quietly growing has no reason to write to you about it, and the provider you did not choose does not know you exist.",
  replication: "craft",
  idea: "The cheaper provider changes over the life of an account, at a balance you can compute in one line, and nobody in the arrangement has any reason to tell you when you crossed it.",
  why: "A proportional charge is close to the only cost in your life that gets larger purely because you succeeded. Nothing about the service changes as a balance triples. The same custody, the same statements, the same website, and the charge triples anyway. A flat fee prices the service. A proportional fee prices your wealth. Both are legitimate business models and only one of them contains a term that grows against you while you sleep.\n\nThe layers matter, because they do not all have the same shape. The investment fee inside the fund is unavoidably proportional and the thing to minimise there is the rate. The administration or platform layer above it is sometimes flat, sometimes proportional with a cap, sometimes proportional with none, and that is where a crossover actually exists. Confusing the two layers is how people end up optimising the wrong one, hunting two basis points inside the wrapper while the wrapper itself takes an uncapped percentage of a balance five times the size it was when they signed up.\n\nThis is an annual job rather than a standing one, and for a precise reason. The crossover balance is fixed the moment you know both schedules. Which side of it you are standing on changes every year you keep contributing. So the instruction is not to choose the cheaper provider, which is a comparison with no end. It is to compute the balance at which the answer flips, write that one number down, and then glance at it once a year.",
  failureMode: "Six years on the platform you picked when the balance was a third of its present size, paying an uncapped percentage on all of it. Nothing went wrong and no market moved against you, and the choice was correct on the day you made it. The cost of never re-running one division is the gap between two fee schedules multiplied by every year since you passed the crossover, and it widens with every contribution.",
  worked: "Two lines, and the first is arithmetic you do exactly once:\n\n\"Write down both fee schedules and compute the balance at which they cross, which is the flat charge divided by the difference between the percentage rates. Put that balance on the same page as my cost ceiling.\"\n\n\"Every year, in the same week as the benchmark check, compare my actual balance with that crossover figure. If I have passed it, move.\"\n\nWhat is deliberately absent is any attempt to keep the comparison live. As long as neither schedule changes, the crossover is a fixed number, so the recurring job is a glance rather than a research exercise. It goes in the same week as the other annual checks because a standalone annual task is a task with no anchor, and those are the ones that quietly stop happening.",
  experiment: "Find the fee schedule for the platform or administration layer holding your largest balance, then find one competitor's. Do the division: the flat charge divided by the difference between the percentage rates gives the balance at which they cross. Write that number down. Compare it with your balance today, and with your balance in five years at your current contribution rate. If you cross it inside five years then the move is already decided and the only open question is when.",
  reflection: "What was your crossover balance, and how far from it are you?",
  recall: {
    q: "A percentage fee and a flat fee are both just prices. Why does the shape of the charge matter more than its level at the moment you choose a provider?",
    a: "Because the level is a fact about today and the shape is a fact about every year afterwards. A flat charge prices the service, which does not change as the balance grows. A proportional charge prices your wealth, so it rises purely because you succeeded, without limit unless it is capped, and the same custody and the same statements cost three times as much once the balance has tripled.\n\nThe consequence is that the cheaper provider changes over the life of the account, at a balance equal to the flat charge divided by the difference between the percentage rates. Nothing prompts the re-comparison, because the provider whose charge is growing has no reason to raise it and the one you did not choose does not know you exist."
  },
  deepDive: "Here are the fee schedules for my current platform and the two alternatives I would consider. Compute the crossover balances, tell me which side of each I am on today, and tell me which one I will cross first at my current contribution rate."
},
{
  id: "costs-turnover-cancels-the-deferral",
  track: "costs", level: "standing",
  title: "Selling a winner turns a deferred liability into a paid one, so turnover costs far more than the brokerage and the difference never appears on a statement",
  source: "The mechanics of a deferred capital gains liability, as they apply to portfolio turnover held outside a tax shelter",
  gateIntro: "You rebalance in April by selling down the holding that has done best, because that is the one that has grown past its weight, and the trade costs nine dollars in brokerage. In October your accountant mentions what you owe on the year's realised gains, and the figure has several more digits in it than the brokerage did. Your portfolio looks almost exactly as it did before you pressed the button.",
  gatePrompt: "Write the instruction that gets your allocation back to target without selling anything, and say what it does with new contributions.",
  evidence: "No trial. This is the mechanics of a deferred liability. An unrealised gain carries a tax obligation that is not payable until you sell, so the money that would otherwise have gone to the revenue stays inside the position and compounds on your behalf in the meantime. Selling ends the arrangement: the liability crystallises, the cash leaves, and everything it would have earned between now and the date you would otherwise have sold leaves with it. Nothing here requires measurement, and the size of the effect depends entirely on your own rate, your holding period and the rules in force, which is why no figure belongs in this entry. What is worth flagging is the accounting asymmetry that keeps it invisible: brokerage prints on the trade confirmation, and the tax appears in a different document months later, attached to a financial year rather than to the decision that caused it.",
  replication: "craft",
  idea: "An unrealised gain is a liability you have been given the free use of, and selling is the act of handing that money over early in exchange for nothing.",
  why: "Think of the deferral as an interest-free loan from the revenue, secured against your position, callable only by you. While the gain sits unrealised, the entire pre-tax amount stays invested and compounds. The moment you sell, you repay that loan and give up the use of the money for however long you would otherwise have gone on holding. The spread and the brokerage are real and they are the small visible portion; the forfeited compounding is the part with no line item.\n\nThis makes turnover different in kind from a fee, not merely different in size. A fee is a percentage of the balance and is at least predictable in advance. The cost of turnover is a function of how much the position has gained, so it grows with success and lands hardest on precisely the holdings you were most right about. It also has no natural units. You cannot quote it as an annual percentage, which is one reason it never appears in anybody's cost comparison, including your own.\n\nThree things follow, and they are the whole practical content. Prefer holdings whose internal turnover is low, because a fund that trades inside the wrapper is spending your deferral for you and telling you about it at the end of the year. Prefer moving new money over selling old money wherever both would reach the same allocation. And treat any rebalance that needs a sale as a decision with a price attached rather than as housekeeping, which is where the next entry starts.",
  failureMode: "An annual rebalance done by selling, for eleven years, on a portfolio held outside any shelter. Each trade felt like housekeeping and cost a few dollars. What it actually did was reset the compounding on the deferred liability eleven separate times, and the amount that never got to compound is not recorded anywhere, on any statement, in any year.",
  worked: "The instruction rebalances with the inflow instead of with the portfolio:\n\n\"Rebalance by direction, not by sale. Each month the automatic contribution goes entirely to whichever holding is furthest below its target weight. I only sell to rebalance if a weight is more than ten percentage points off target and the inflow cannot close the gap inside a year.\"\n\nAnd the holding rule underneath it:\n\n\"Outside a shelter I hold things with low internal turnover, and I check the capital gains distributed on last year's statement before buying any fund that trades a lot.\"\n\nWhat is deliberately absent is a calendar rebalancing date. Dating the rebalance guarantees a sale in the years when the inflow alone would have been enough, and the band plus the inflow rule arrives at roughly the same allocation without the trade.",
  experiment: "Look at your last twelve months of trades outside super and add up the gains they realised. Then add up the brokerage on the same trades. The ratio between those two figures is the part of turnover cost you have been ignoring, and for most people the two are not remotely close. Then change your next contribution so that it goes entirely to the underweight holding rather than splitting evenly, which is one dropdown and removes the reason for the next sale.",
  reflection: "What did last year's trades cost in realised gains, next to what they cost in brokerage?",
  forecast: { q: "Will I get through the next twelve months without selling anything outside super purely in order to rebalance?" },
  recall: {
    q: "Brokerage on a rebalancing trade is trivial, so why is turnover one of the larger controllable costs in a portfolio held outside a shelter?",
    a: "Because the sale crystallises a tax liability that had been deferred. While the gain is unrealised the whole pre-tax amount stays in the position and compounds for you, which is the free use of money you would otherwise have handed over. Selling repays it early and forfeits everything it would have earned between now and the date you would have sold anyway. The brokerage is the visible and much smaller half.\n\nIt is also invisible in a way a fee is not. Brokerage prints on the trade confirmation while the tax turns up months later attached to a financial year rather than to the trade that caused it, and the cost cannot be expressed as a tidy annual percentage, so it never enters a cost comparison."
  },
  deepDive: "Here is my portfolio outside super with the cost bases. Tell me how to get back to my target weights using contributions only, and what doing it by selling instead would have cost me in crystallised gains."
},
{
  id: "costs-factor-fee-is-certain",
  track: "costs", level: "judgement",
  title: "The fee on a tilt is contractual and the premium it is sold on is a forecast, so you are trading a certainty for an estimate",
  source: "The pricing of factor and smart beta products against the published premia used to sell them, alongside the multiple-testing problem in the factor literature",
  gateIntro: "The product page shows a chart running back to 1975 in which the tilt finishes above the plain index, and a fee four times what the broad fund on the same platform charges. Both of those numbers are true. You have ninety seconds before a meeting, you were going to add it to the regular contribution, and there is a button on the page that would do it now.",
  gatePrompt: "Write the instruction that decides whether the extra fee gets paid, in a form you could apply to the next three products somebody shows you.",
  evidence: "Two established things placed next to each other. The fee is contractual: it is written in the disclosure document, it is charged on the whole balance every year, and it does not depend on the strategy working. The premium is an estimate drawn from a historical series, and the factor literature has a well-documented multiple-testing problem that the entry on published premia in the evidence track handles properly. This entry is not making that argument again. It is the narrower observation that the two sides of the trade have completely different epistemic status while product marketing sets them in the same typeface. No trial is cited for the asymmetry, because it is not the kind of claim a trial addresses. The verdict is overclaimed rather than absent because many of these premia are real in the samples they were measured on and several have plausible economic stories behind them. The overclaim is in the confidence attached to the forward-looking number.",
  replication: "overclaimed",
  idea: "Paying more for a tilt swaps a guaranteed annual subtraction for an expectation over an unspecified horizon, so the expectation has to be worth a great deal more than the subtraction costs.",
  why: "Grant the best case first. Suppose the premium is entirely real and the published point estimate is exactly right. You still pay the fee every year with total certainty, while collecting the premium as a single draw from a distribution wide enough that a decade can pass without it turning up. The spread between the tilt's charge and the broad fund's charge is a known annual subtraction. The compensation is an expectation over a horizon nobody in the brochure has specified, and your holding period is not infinite.\n\nThen apply the two discounts the forward number has earned. The first is selection: the published figure is the estimate that a heavily searched literature produced, and estimates chosen for being large are biased upward, which is the statistical argument the evidence track makes properly. The second is crowding, and it is independent of the first. A premium that is real and widely known attracts money, and the compensation for bearing a risk that everybody has now bought is lower than the compensation measured in the years before they bought it. Both discounts apply to the uncertain side of the trade. Neither applies to the fee.\n\nThat gives a decision rule that terminates, which is the point of putting this in the controllable set. Do not try to settle whether the factor is real, because that argument does not end. Ask whether you would still pay this spread over the plain index fee if the backtest did not exist and you had only the economic story. If the answer is no, the chart is doing the persuading, and the chart is the weakest part of the case about the future. The rule does not say a tilt is never worth buying. It puts the burden where it belongs.",
  failureMode: "Four times the plain fund's fee, paid every year for nine years, in exchange for a premium the chart showed and the period you actually held did not deliver. You may well still be right about the next twenty years. The fee was never conditional on you being right, and it has already been charged nine times.",
  worked: "The instruction is a test you apply before the button:\n\n\"Before paying more than my index fee for any tilt, write the sentence that would justify it if the backtest did not exist. If I cannot write that sentence, I buy the cheap broad fund instead.\"\n\nAnd the containment, because occasionally the test will pass:\n\n\"Anything that passes goes inside the ten percent active cap, funded out of new contributions, with a review date and that sentence attached to it.\"\n\nWhat is deliberately absent is any rule about which factors are genuine. That argument does not terminate and it does not need to, because the test only has to establish whether the chart is the thing doing the persuading.",
  experiment: "Take the last product you were tempted by, or the one you already own, and write the justifying sentence without referring to a single historical return figure. Then look up its fee and your broad fund's fee and write down the annual difference against the amount you would actually allocate. If the sentence is thin and the difference is not, you have your answer. If the sentence is genuinely good, put the product inside your active cap where it belongs rather than in the core.",
  reflection: "Could you write the justifying sentence without the chart, and what did the fee difference come to?",
  recall: {
    q: "Suppose a factor premium is entirely real. Why is that still not sufficient reason to pay a higher fee to capture it?",
    a: "Because the two sides of the trade differ in kind. The fee is contractual, charged on the whole balance every year whether the strategy works or not. The premium is an expectation over an unspecified horizon, drawn from a distribution wide enough that a decade can pass without it appearing. You are exchanging a certainty for an estimate, so the estimate has to be worth considerably more than the certainty costs.\n\nThe forward-looking number also carries two separate discounts. One for selection, because the published figure is the estimate that a heavily searched literature produced. One for crowding, because a premium that is real and known attracts money, and the compensation for a risk everybody has already bought is lower than the compensation measured before they bought it. Both discounts apply to the uncertain side of the trade and neither touches the fee."
  },
  deepDive: "Here is the tilt I am considering and here is my current core holding with its fee. Write the strongest case for the tilt that does not use a backtest, then attack that case, and tell me what the fee difference costs me over twenty years if it turns out to be wrong."
},
{
  id: "costs-rebalancing-buys-risk-control",
  track: "costs", level: "annual",
  title: "Rebalancing reliably buys you the risk level you chose and unreliably buys extra return, and it is sold almost entirely as the second one",
  source: "The rebalancing bonus as it appears in adviser and platform material, against the conditions the arithmetic behind it actually requires",
  gateIntro: "It is the first week of July and your growth holdings have drifted eight points above target, because equities had a good year and nothing else did. The article open in front of you calls the correction a rebalancing bonus and shows a chart where the corrected portfolio finishes ahead. Selling down the winner would crystallise a gain, and your cursor is over the button.",
  gatePrompt: "Write the instruction that decides when a drift gets corrected, with the band and the method both written into it.",
  evidence: "No trial, and an overclaim rather than a finding. The rebalancing bonus is real in the arithmetic under conditions that marketing material leaves unstated: the assets need broadly similar long-run returns and enough independent movement, and the gain arrives by smoothing the compound path rather than by lifting anybody's expected return. Where one asset persistently out-earns the other, a mechanical rule sells the better asset to buy the worse one and the bonus becomes a cost. Simulation studies of rebalancing frequency exist in quantity and their answers swing on the period and the asset pair chosen, which is a reliable sign that no general result is available. What can be asserted without any study is what rebalancing definitely does, which is hold your risk exposure near the level you picked. That is a genuinely valuable service, and it is not the service being advertised.",
  replication: "overclaimed",
  idea: "Correcting a drift delivers the risk you chose with certainty and extra return only under conditions you cannot verify in advance, and the sales material has those two the wrong way round.",
  why: "Start with what is certain. Left alone, a portfolio drifts toward whatever has grown, which means your exposure to the risky asset rises after it has been rewarding and falls after it has fallen. That drift is not neutral. Following a long run in equities, the untouched portfolio is more aggressive than the one you signed up for, at the point in the cycle where you have the least margin for it to go wrong. Correcting the drift is the mechanism that keeps the allocation you wrote down and the allocation you own as the same thing, and that is the whole reliable benefit.\n\nNow the uncertain part. The arithmetic that produces the bonus requires the assets to have broadly similar long-run returns and to move somewhat independently, and it operates on the variance of the compound path rather than on expected return. Break either condition, which is to say let one asset systematically out-earn the other, and the rule becomes an instruction to sell the better asset in order to buy the worse one, repeatedly, for as long as you keep running it. Since you do not know in advance which regime you are in, the bonus is a forecast dressed in the clothes of arithmetic.\n\nThe mislabelling is expensive for a specific reason. If you believe the correction produces return, you do it often, because more of a good thing is better. If you believe it produces risk control, you do it rarely and with a band, because control is only needed once the drift is large enough to matter and every correction is paid for in tax and spread. The same portfolio and the same evidence generate opposite frequencies depending on which story you hold, and the story being sold is the expensive one.",
  failureMode: "Four corrections a year for six years, each one crystallising a gain and each one justified by the bonus. The portfolio finished within a point or two of where an annual band would have left it, and the difference between the two paths is almost entirely the tax and the spread on the fifteen extra trades.",
  worked: "A band rather than a date, and it names both numbers:\n\n\"I correct only when growth assets are more than ten percentage points away from seventy, and I close the gap with contributions first, selling only if contributions cannot do it inside a year.\"\n\nThen the sentence that stops the band being renegotiated:\n\n\"The band exists to hold my risk at the level I chose. It is not a return strategy, so a year in which leaving the drift alone would have done better is not evidence against it.\"\n\nWhat is deliberately absent is a frequency. Naming a date guarantees a trade in the years when nothing has drifted far enough to matter. The second sentence is there because in a good many years the band will look as though it cost you something, which is exactly what a risk control looks like from the inside.",
  experiment: "Work out your current drift from target in percentage points, then write your band down as a number rather than carrying it as a feeling. Ten points is a defensible default, and any band you will honour beats a cleverer one you will not. Then check whether your platform can direct incoming contributions to the underweight holding automatically. Several can, and switching that on converts this entry from an annual decision into something that happens without you.",
  reflection: "What is your band, and were you inside it or outside it when you checked?",
  forecast: { q: "Will my next rebalance be triggered by my own band rather than by a market movement I read about?" },
  recall: {
    q: "Rebalancing is sold as a source of extra return. What does it reliably provide instead, and under what condition does the return story reverse?",
    a: "It reliably holds your risk exposure near the level you chose. A portfolio left alone drifts toward whatever has grown, so after a long run in the risky asset the untouched version is more aggressive than the one you designed, at the point in the cycle with the least margin for error. That is the service being bought.\n\nThe return story reverses when the assets have persistently different long-run returns. The arithmetic behind the bonus needs roughly similar returns and some independence of movement, and it works by smoothing the compound path rather than raising expected return. If one asset systematically out-earns the other, the rule sells the better one to buy the worse one for as long as it keeps running."
  },
  deepDive: "Here is my target allocation and my actual balances. Set a band I will honour, tell me whether contributions alone can close my current drift, and tell me what fixing it by selling would cost me instead."
},
{
  id: "costs-hedging-is-a-cost",
  track: "costs", level: "standing",
  title: "Currency hedging is a price paid to remove a risk rather than a strategy, and for an Australian it can remove the one that was helping",
  source: "The pricing of forward exchange rates off the interest differential, together with the observed behaviour of the Australian dollar in global risk-off episodes",
  gateIntro: "There are two versions of the same global shares fund on your platform, hedged and unhedged, and the hedged one costs slightly more. You have no view on the Australian dollar and you feel faintly embarrassed about that. The regular contribution has to point at one of them by Friday, and whichever you choose you will not look at again for a decade.",
  gatePrompt: "Write the instruction that picks one of the two for the next decade, and name the thing that would make you change it.",
  evidence: "No trial, and two claims of very different strength. The first is close to arithmetic: forward exchange rates are priced off the interest differential between the two currencies, so hedging is not free and its running cost is roughly that differential plus whatever the manager charges to operate it. It removes variance rather than adding expectation, because there is no reason a currency should pay you for holding it across decades. The second claim is empirical and much weaker. The Australian dollar has behaved as a high-beta currency, tending to fall when global markets fall, so an unhedged foreign holding has historically seen part of its drawdowns offset by the currency translating in your favour. That is a regularity across the floating-rate era rather than a law, it depends on Australia's terms of trade and rate differentials continuing to behave as they have, and it could stop. Anybody quoting you a figure for the size of that offset is quoting one period.",
  replication: "craft",
  idea: "Hedging changes the variance of a foreign holding without changing its expected return, which makes it a purchase rather than a strategy, and the cover it sells may not be the cover you need.",
  why: "Hedging cannot add return, and the reason is structural rather than empirical. Forward rates are set by the interest differential, so locking one in is not a way to make money systematically, and over decades a currency is not an asset that pays you to hold it. What the hedge buys is the removal of the currency's contribution to variance. What it costs is the differential plus the fee for running the programme. So it is priced like insurance, and the right question is whether you want the cover, not whether the mechanism works.\n\nFor an Australian holding global equities the answer is genuinely not obvious, because the currency exposure has not historically behaved like added noise. It has behaved like a partial hedge. The dollar has tended to fall when global markets fall, which translates the foreign holding upward in your own currency at the moment you least want a large drawdown, so the unhedged version has had shallower falls in local terms than the underlying market did. Paying to remove that leaves you with a purer equity exposure and a worse worst case. Note the tense throughout: this is a regularity, not a mechanism guaranteed to persist.\n\nThe useful distinction is about which asset the hedge is wrapped around. On long-horizon foreign equity it is a preference and a reasonable person can go either way. On a foreign defensive holding it is closer to a requirement, because currency variance can be several times the variance of the asset itself, and an unhedged bond holding is mostly a currency position wearing a bond's name. And on money you intend to spend in Australian dollars within a few years, the question does not arise, because that money should not be offshore.",
  failureMode: "A decade in the hedged version of a global fund because the word hedged sounded like the prudent choice, paying the differential and the extra management charge every year, and ending with a portfolio whose worst case in your own currency is worse than the cheaper option would have given you. None of this shows up as an error in any single year, which is exactly why nobody goes back to it.",
  worked: "Two lines, and the second is the one that keeps it settled:\n\n\"Foreign equity goes in the unhedged version. Anything defensive that is denominated in a foreign currency goes in the hedged version. Money I intend to spend in Australian dollars within three years does not go offshore at all.\"\n\n\"This is a structural choice and not a currency view, so a year in which the dollar moves against me is not a reason to switch.\"\n\nWhat is deliberately absent is any forecast for the exchange rate, and the instruction is built so that it never requires one. The second line exists because the first will look wrong for entire years at a time, and switching after a bad run is how a settled decision becomes an expensive one.",
  experiment: "Check which version of each foreign holding you actually own, because a surprising number of people do not know and the difference is one word in the fund's name. Then check the fee gap between the two versions on your platform. If your foreign equity is hedged and there is no reason for it written down anywhere, that is a decision you made by accident, and it is one dropdown to change on future contributions without touching what you already hold.",
  reflection: "Which version were you in, and had you chosen it deliberately?",
  recall: {
    q: "Why can hedging not raise the expected return on a foreign holding, and what does that make it?",
    a: "Because forward exchange rates are priced off the interest differential between the two currencies, so locking a rate in is not a systematic source of gain, and a currency is not an asset that pays you to hold it over decades. The hedge removes the currency's contribution to variance and charges you the differential plus a management fee to do it, which makes it a purchase priced like insurance. The question is whether you want the cover.\n\nFor an Australian holding global equities the answer is not obvious, because the dollar has tended to fall when global markets fall, which translates a foreign holding upward in local currency exactly when that helps. Paying to strip that out buys a purer equity exposure and a worse worst case. On a foreign defensive holding the calculation flips, because currency variance can be several times the asset's own and defeats the reason for holding it."
  },
  deepDive: "Here are my foreign holdings and which version of each I own. Tell me where hedging is earning its price in my portfolio and where I am paying for cover I did not want, and give me one sentence to record so I stop revisiting it."
},
{
  id: "costs-advice-is-barely-customised",
  track: "costs", level: "judgement",
  title: "Which adviser you happen to get explains more about your portfolio's risk than anything about you does",
  source: "Stephen Foerster, Juhani Linnainmaa, Brian Melzer and Alessandro Previtero, Retail Financial Advice: Does One Size Fit All? (Journal of Finance, 2017)",
  gateIntro: "The recommendation arrives and it is a perfectly reasonable portfolio. In the waiting room you had spent twenty minutes on a questionnaire about your horizon, your dependants and how you would feel about a thirty percent fall. Your friend, who earns half what you do, has no children and is eleven years younger, saw the same adviser and came out with a document that looks remarkably like yours.",
  gatePrompt: "Write the instruction that would tell you, in one afternoon a year, whether the portfolio you were handed is the one your own circumstances call for.",
  evidence: "Administrative records from several Canadian financial institutions covering tens of thousands of clients and thousands of advisers, with each client's portfolio risk decomposed into the part explained by the client's own characteristics and the part explained by the identity of their adviser. Client attributes including age, stated risk tolerance, income and horizon accounted for a small share of the variation in portfolio risk. Which adviser a client happened to be assigned accounted for substantially more. The second result is what changes the interpretation: advisers' own personal portfolios closely resembled the ones they recommended, and advisers who took more risk with their own money had clients who took more risk. Limitations: one country, one distribution model, and a period in which most of these advisers were paid by commission, so it does not transport cleanly to a fee-for-service arrangement. It measures risk taken rather than outcomes, and it cannot say whether any particular recommendation was wrong for the person who received it.",
  replication: "single",
  idea: "Advice is far less customised than the intake process implies, and the pattern looks less like a conflict of interest than like one worldview being applied to everybody, the adviser included.",
  why: "The obvious reading of weak customisation is dishonesty, and the second finding rules it out. If advisers were steering clients into arrangements that suited the adviser while keeping something better for themselves, their own portfolios would look different from their recommendations. They did not. They mirrored them, and advisers who took more risk personally had clients who took more risk. The economical explanation is that each adviser holds a view about how a portfolio ought to look, applies it to every client and to their own money, and the questionnaire you filled in is largely a compliance artefact rather than an input to anything.\n\nThat is worse news than a conflict would have been. A conflict of interest is detectable and legislatable: you can ask who is paid what, and jurisdictions have. A sincerely held one-size view is invisible from outside, survives a ban on commissions entirely, and is delivered with complete conviction, which is precisely what makes it persuasive. The customisation is the part the process advertises most loudly and, on this evidence, delivers least.\n\nFor the controllable set the implication is direct. Allocation is the decision that carries the level of your return, and this says the allocation you receive is drawn substantially from a distribution over advisers rather than a distribution over clients. The facts that ought to drive it, which are how secure your income actually is, what your horizon actually is, and how much of your net worth sits in one illiquid asset, are the facts you have to bring in writing yourself. That is not an argument against taking advice. It is an argument that the allocation is yours, and that a second opinion is only worth having from somebody with a different worldview rather than the same one.",
  failureMode: "Eleven years in a portfolio suited to somebody with a different income, a different horizon and no dependants, because it is the portfolio that adviser gives everybody. You cannot detect this from the returns, because it will look like a normal portfolio doing normal things the entire time, and you find out when the horizon that was never yours arrives.",
  worked: "The instruction is a written brief that goes in before the recommendation comes out:\n\n\"Before any advice meeting, write one page: my horizon in years, what fraction of my net worth sits in one illiquid asset, how secure my income is, and the size of the fall that would actually make me sell. Hand it over, and ask which of those four changed the recommendation.\"\n\nThen the annual version, for advice you already hold:\n\n\"Once a year, re-read that page and ask whether the portfolio I own matches it. If two of the four have changed and the portfolio has not, that is the meeting to book.\"\n\nWhat is deliberately absent is any judgement about the adviser. The question is not whether they are good at the job. It is whether anything specific to you entered the recommendation, and asking which of the four moved it is answerable in one sentence.",
  experiment: "Write the one-page brief today, before you need it: horizon in years, illiquid concentration as a percentage, income security in a sentence, and the drawdown at which you would genuinely sell. It takes about fifteen minutes and it will be the only version of your own circumstances that exists in writing anywhere. Then hold it up against the portfolio you currently hold and see whether the two are related at all.",
  reflection: "Which of the four items on your page had anybody ever asked you about, and does your portfolio reflect any of them?",
  recall: {
    q: "Advisers' recommendations show little customisation to client circumstances. Why does the finding that advisers hold similar portfolios themselves make this more concerning rather than less?",
    a: "Because it rules out the explanation that would have been easiest to fix. If advisers were steering clients into arrangements while holding something better themselves, that is a conflict of interest, and conflicts are detectable and can be legislated against. Instead their own portfolios mirrored their advice, and advisers who took more risk personally had clients who took more risk.\n\nWhat is left is a sincerely held one-size view applied to everybody including the adviser. That survives a ban on commissions, is invisible from outside, and comes with total conviction, which is what makes it persuasive. The consequence is that the facts which should drive your allocation, meaning your real horizon, your income security and your illiquid concentration, are ones you have to bring in writing yourself."
  },
  deepDive: "Here is my one-page brief and here is the portfolio I hold. Tell me which of those four facts about me the portfolio actually reflects, and which of them nobody has ever asked about."
},
{
  id: "costs-decide-once-then-defend",
  track: "costs", level: "judgement",
  title: "Every decision in the controllable set is made once, and the whole remaining skill is refusing to remake it",
  source: "The practice of writing an investment policy statement and holding it against yourself, which institutional trustees are obliged to do and individuals almost never do",
  gateIntro: "It is the fourth week of a bad quarter. Your allocation is unchanged, your costs are the lowest they have ever been, and there is nothing on your list to do about any of it. Somebody on a podcast is making a specific and genuinely plausible argument about the next two years, the platform is open in another tab, and there is nothing at all stopping you.",
  gatePrompt: "Write the instruction that governs what you are allowed to do in a week like that one, and name who has to countersign it.",
  evidence: "No trial. This is the discipline that institutional investing formalised as a policy statement and that individuals almost never write down: the allocation, the cost ceiling, the band, the conditions under which any of them may change, and the date of the next review, all recorded before the conditions arrive. There is no randomised evidence that individuals who write one end up wealthier, and the design would be difficult, because people who write policy statements differ from people who do not in every way that matters. What is documented elsewhere in this corpus is the failure mode it targets, which is that changes made during a drawdown are made on worse information than the original decision and are rarely reversed on the way back up. Treat it as a practice with a plausible mechanism and nothing behind it.",
  replication: "craft",
  idea: "The controllable set is a short list of decisions each settled in a single afternoon, so after that afternoon the only skill left is declining to reopen them.",
  why: "Count the list. The savings rate and its escalation. The cost ceiling. The allocation. The correction band. Where each dollar sits. The hedging choice. The turnover rule. Seven decisions, all of them settleable in an afternoon or two, and all of them finished once settled. Not one improves by being revisited, and every one gets worse when revisited under pressure, because the pressure is correlated with the market rather than with any new information about you.\n\nThat is the strange asymmetry of this whole domain. The work is front-loaded to the point of being almost trivial, and then the job changes character completely. It stops being a decision problem, where being numerate is an advantage, and becomes a defence problem, where it is not. The forecastable half supplies the ammunition: there is always a plausible argument available, it is always specific and current, and it always arrives carrying more urgency than a written band does.\n\nWriting it down is the mechanism rather than the record, and this is the part people skip. A decision held in your head has no fixed earlier version, so when you revise it you cannot tell revision from drift, and you will reliably reconstruct your former self as having agreed with you. A dated page with the reason on it makes revision visible and therefore expensive. It does not require willpower. It only requires the earlier version to be legible.\n\nAnd the honest limit, which is why this is last in the track rather than first: none of it can be handed to an instruction. Every other entry here ends with something a system does while you are not watching. This one ends with a page you have to reread, which is the argument for writing it in a form that shames you slightly.",
  failureMode: "One decision reopened in the fourth week of a bad quarter and never closed again. The allocation came down five points, the reasoning was excellent at the time, and it did not go back up when the market did, because no page existed saying what it had been. Six years later the portfolio is more defensive than the one you designed and nobody ever chose that.",
  worked: "One page, dated, short enough to reread in two minutes:\n\n\"Savings rate: this amount, escalating on the day my pay changes. Cost ceiling: this percentage. Allocation: these percentages. Band: ten points. Hedging: unhedged for equity, hedged for defensive. Next review: the first of July. Any change needs a written reason and a date, and a market movement is not a reason.\"\n\nThen the countersignature, which is the only part of this with teeth:\n\n\"Any change to this page gets sent to my partner before it takes effect, with the reason written out.\"\n\nWhat is deliberately absent is anything at all about markets. The page contains only quantities you set, which is why it stays true on the worst day, and the requirement to send it to somebody exists because a rule you can amend silently is not a rule.",
  experiment: "Write the page today, in whatever form you will actually reread, and put the date on it. Seven lines is enough, and most people manage it in twenty minutes because every decision on the list has already been made and simply never recorded. Then send it to one other person. Not for their approval, for the record: the point of a second copy is that your future self cannot revise it without somebody noticing.",
  reflection: "How long did the page take, and which line did you discover you had never actually decided?",
  forecast: { q: "Will the page I write today still say the same thing in twelve months, and will I be able to prove it?" },
  recall: {
    q: "Every decision in the controllable set can be made in an afternoon, so why is this the hardest part of the track to actually run?",
    a: "Because after that afternoon the problem changes character. It stops being a decision problem, where being numerate helps, and becomes a defence problem, where it does not. The forecastable half supplies an endless stream of plausible, specific, current arguments for reopening a settled question, and they arrive with far more urgency than a written band ever does.\n\nWriting it down is the mechanism and not the record. A decision held only in your head has no fixed earlier version, so revision is indistinguishable from drift and you will reconstruct your former self as having agreed with you. A dated page with a reason on it makes revision visible and therefore costly, and sending a copy to somebody else is what stops the page from being amended quietly."
  },
  deepDive: "Here are my seven controllable decisions as they stand today. Turn them into a one-page policy I could reread in two minutes, and tell me which line I am most likely to break first and what would trigger it."
}
);
